// =============================================================================
// MemQL Shape Reference
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for `shape` syntax. It is NOT loaded by
// the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to
// skip it). Every shape-level annotation, every body form, every kind
// (concept-bound, trait, caller, mixed), and every rejected form is
// demonstrated below with inline commentary.
//
// A shape is a projection template. Queries and mutations apply shapes to
// rows; specs bind to shapes to express predicates over projected fields.
// Shapes have NO inputs, NO outputs -- the body is a list of field paths
// and optional `include` statements.
//
// =============================================================================
//   1. Shape-level annotations
// =============================================================================
//
// Every annotation goes above the `shape` keyword. The full author surface
// is THREE annotations:
//
//   @description("text")    Human-readable description.       optional
//   @row                    Kind marker: projects row fields. optional*
//   @actor                 Kind marker: projects auth envelope. optional*
//
//   (* a shape must carry at least one of @row / @actor. Both is
//      allowed but no current users exist.)
//
// The `@useConcept(...)` family was retired in the import-model pivot.
// The concept binding now lives in the SIGNATURE (`shape <Concept> <name>`),
// not in an annotation. Anything outside the allow-list (and the legacy
// `@use*` family in particular) is rejected at parse time with a
// migration-pointing error.
//
// =============================================================================
//   2. Canonical concept-bound shape -- the most common case
// =============================================================================
//
// A concept-bound shape declares `@row` (project row fields) and binds
// the source concept via its SIGNATURE: `shape <Concept> <name>`. The
// body lists the row intrinsics and payload fields to project. Body
// translations:
//
//   row.X            -> row intrinsic (id, createdAt, createdBy, etc.)
//   payload.X        -> payload field
//
// The bare-name dotted-prefix form (`participant.X`) is retired; payload
// fields are always written as `payload.X` post-migration.

@row
@description("Comprehensive participant projection.")
shape participant participantFull {
  row.id
  payload.partitionId
  payload.userId
  payload.displayName
  payload.status
  row.createdAt
  row.createdBy
}

// =============================================================================
//   3. Trait shape -- concept-agnostic row scaffold
// =============================================================================
//
// A trait shape is `@row` WITHOUT a signature-bound concept. It declares
// row fields that any concept might have. Specs bind to trait shapes via
// the spec's `@shape("<name>")` annotation to express predicates that
// apply across many concepts.
//
// Trait shapes shipped under `dsl/common/shapes.memql`:
//   activeRowTrait      payload.active
//   statusRowTrait      payload.status
//   deletedRowTrait     payload.deleted
//   archivedRowTrait    payload.archivedAt
//   savedRowTrait       payload.savedAt
//   validationRowTrait  payload.validationStatus + checkedAt + confirmedAt

@row
@description("Concept-agnostic scaffold for predicates that check payload.active.")
shape activeRowTrait {
  payload.active
}

@row
@description("Concept-agnostic scaffold for predicates that check payload.status.")
shape statusRowTrait {
  payload.status
}

// =============================================================================
//   4. Caller shape -- auth envelope projection
// =============================================================================
//
// A caller shape declares `@actor` (project auth envelope) instead of
// `@row`. Body paths reference `actor.*` fields. The canonical example
// is `actorEnvelope`, used by every context-spec via the spec's
// `@shape("actorEnvelope")` binding.

@actor
@description("Caller envelope projection: authenticated actor, role, and now.")
shape actorEnvelope {
  actor.userId
  actor.role
  actor.identityId
  actor.isClusterOwner
  actor.now
}

// =============================================================================
//   5. Mixed @row + @actor -- both worlds
// =============================================================================
//
// A shape MAY carry both kind markers. Body paths can then reference
// both row fields and caller fields. The engine accepts this; no
// current users exist.

@row
@actor
@description("Hypothetical mixed projection -- row + caller envelope.")
shape participant participantWithCallerContext {
  row.id
  payload.displayName
  actor.userId
  actor.role
}

// =============================================================================
//   6. Body path syntax
// =============================================================================
//
// Each line in the body is one of:
//
//   <fieldPath>           Project the named field.
//   include <otherShape>  Include every field from another shape.
//   // <comment>          Inline comment -- ignored by the parser.
//
// Field path translation rules:
//
//   row.X                Row intrinsic (no `payload.` prefix). Renders
//                        in the projection under key X.
//                        Intrinsics: id, createdAt, createdBy, concept,
//                        partition, type, schema.
//
//   payload.X            Payload field reference. Renders under key X.
//                        This is the only legal way to reference a
//                        payload field -- the legacy bare-prefix form
//                        (`<conceptName>.X`) is rejected at load time
//                        by the legacy-filter-form validator.
//
//   actor.X             Auth-envelope reference. Requires @actor.

@row
@description("Demonstrates the body path forms in one place.")
shape participant pathSyntaxExample {
  row.id                            // intrinsic
  row.createdAt                     // intrinsic
  payload.partitionId                   // payload field
  payload.displayName               // payload field
  payload.status                    // payload field
}

// =============================================================================
//   7. EXPLICITLY REJECTED on shapes
// =============================================================================
//
//   @useConcept(<bareName>)
//   @useShape(...)
//   @useQuery(...) / @useMutation(...) / @useLogic(...) / @useBuiltin(...)
//     Retired in the import-model pivot. Bind concepts via the signature
//     (`shape <Concept> <name>`); pull cross-file constructs via a
//     file-top `use <module>.{ ... }` import.
//
//   @concepts("v1:foo:bar")
//     The legacy fully-qualified-id binding. Retired. Bind via the
//     signature; do not write concept ids in annotations.
//
//   Any unknown annotation
//     The parser hard-rejects with the full allow-list in the error.
//     This is intentional: typos surface as load-time errors, not as
//     silent no-ops.

// =============================================================================
//   8. The legacy `func (Shape) name(...) { ... }` form -- retired
// =============================================================================
//
// The struct-form `shape <Concept> <name> { ... }` (or, for trait /
// caller shapes, the single-identifier `shape <name> { ... }`) is the
// only shape declaration the parser accepts. The procedural
// function-receiver form was retired during the consolidation.
// Attempting it produces a parser receiver-type error.
//
//     // NOT VALID -- struct form is required.
//     func (Shape) participantFull() { ... }

// =============================================================================
//   9. How shapes get consumed
// =============================================================================
//
//   - From a query's `shape` clause:
//
//         use cognition.shapes.{ participantFull }
//
//         @description("Get space participants")
//         query participant spaceParticipants {
//           args   { partitionId string @required }
//           filter payload.partitionId == args.partitionId
//           shape  participantFull
//         }
//
//   - From a spec's `@shape("...")` binding:
//
//         use common.shapes.{ actorEnvelope }
//
//         @shape("actorEnvelope")
//         spec requiresAdmin {
//           actor.role == "admin"
//         }
//
//     The shape must exist at engine startup. The post-load
//     ValidateSpecBindings pass enforces this and errors with
//
//          `spec %q binds @shape(%s) but no such shape is registered`
//
//     when a referenced shape is missing.
//
//   - From a mutation's `insert` / `update` body: payload field
//     references inside the mutation correspond to the shape's
//     projected fields.
